window.academyTextbookV1={"flight":[{"module":0,"title":"Textbook Unit 1 — Flight as an energy and forces system","level":1,"objectives":["Distinguish the four aerodynamic forces and relate their balances to acceleration","Distinguish pitch attitude, flight-path angle and angle of attack","Apply a simplified lift equation with correct units and assumptions","Explain why performance changes with density and loading","Evaluate an apparently successful simulator takeoff without confusing it with real-world qualification"],"sections":[["Forces, velocity and acceleration","An airplane is subject to weight, aerodynamic lift, drag and propulsive thrust. These are vectors, so direction matters as much as magnitude. In a steady, unaccelerated idealized level flight condition, the net force is approximately zero, but forces are still present. Thrust counteracts drag and wing lift primarily opposes weight. A pilot cannot infer that the airplane is in equilibrium from one instrument number, because acceleration is determined by the vector sum of forces and by the airplane's mass.","When thrust exceeds the drag opposing forward motion, the aircraft may accelerate. When lift alone no longer supports the required flight path, the airplane's trajectory changes. The real aircraft also has pitching, rolling and yawing moments. Those moments depend on control surfaces, aerodynamic stability, center of gravity and airflow. Introductory teaching diagrams often group all aerodynamic lift into one arrow through a convenient location; actual force distributions are more complex."],["Angle of attack versus nose attitude","Pitch attitude is the nose's orientation relative to an external reference such as the horizon. Angle of attack is the difference between the wing chord direction and the relative airflow. Flight-path angle describes the aircraft's velocity direction relative to the horizon. These three angles can differ considerably. An airplane may have its nose above the horizon while descending, for example when its flight path falls more steeply than its nose points.","The wing's lift coefficient usually changes with angle of attack over its normal operating range. At sufficiently high angle of attack, airflow separation can reduce lift and produce a stall. A stall is not defined by a particular altitude, throttle setting or universal airspeed. Aircraft loading, configuration and maneuvering affect the indicated speed at which a critical angle of attack may be reached. This conceptual relationship matters more than memorizing an isolated 'stall speed'."],["A model of lift and its limits","A common instructional approximation is L = ½ ρ V² S CL. Here L is lift in newtons, ρ is air density in kg/m³, V is true airspeed in m/s, S is wing area in m² and CL is a dimensionless lift coefficient. The model illustrates how lift varies with speed squared when other quantities remain constant. However CL is not automatically constant when the airplane changes attitude or configuration; it depends strongly on angle of attack and other conditions.","Suppose density is 1.2 kg/m³, speed 40 m/s, wing area 16 m² and CL 0.5. Then lift = 0.5 × 1.2 × 40² × 16 × 0.5 = 7,680 N. If speed decreases to 30 m/s and all else unrealistically stays unchanged, lift becomes 4,320 N. This is a teaching calculation, not a prediction of a specific aircraft's behavior. In real flight the wing's coefficient, thrust and trajectory may all change."],["Energy, climb and density altitude","Kinetic energy is ½ mV²; gravitational potential energy near Earth's surface is approximately mgh. During a power-limited climb, a gain in altitude may draw on kinetic energy, so airspeed can decline. Sustained climb depends on excess available power after overcoming drag and other losses. Aerodynamic drag itself changes with speed and configuration, so managing energy is not as simple as moving the yoke toward a particular angle.","Density altitude helps express how low-density air affects aircraft performance. High temperature and elevation can reduce the lift and propulsion available at a given true airspeed. Takeoff and climb performance are therefore sensitive to atmosphere, loading, wind, runway slope and condition. Actual performance planning needs the specific aircraft's approved procedures and charts. Our app's simplified flight simulation demonstrates relationships but cannot validate a real takeoff distance or safe flight decision."],["Interpreting training feedback","Consider an introductory simulator takeoff in which the numerical airspeed rises and the drawn aircraft climbs. Those observations may show that the software implements a functioning motion model; they do not prove that the model represents actual aerodynamics. A responsible evaluation asks which equations are implemented, whether the numerical integration is stable, whether airspeed is indicated or true, and whether liftoff and stall behavior have been calibrated against trustworthy aircraft-specific data.","A useful learning journal records an initial hypothesis, observed states, model assumptions and a revised explanation. This helps the student avoid confusing an attractive cockpit or a high score with professional readiness. Real aviation competency requires approved flight instruction, formal standards and supervised practical experience."]],"worked":"Worked problem: A conceptual wing generates 7,680 N of lift using ρ=1.2 kg/m³, V=40 m/s, S=16 m², and CL=0.5. If V changes to 30 m/s and all other inputs are held constant, calculate 4,320 N. The lift ratio is (30/40)²=0.5625. Then explain why holding CL constant is a modeling assumption that may fail when the airplane actually pitches or changes flight path.","questions":[["Which directly defines an aerodynamic stall?",["Passing a critical angle of attack","Descending below 1,000 feet","Any reduction in throttle"],0,"A stall is associated with exceeding critical angle of attack, regardless of a single universal airspeed."]],"optionalPrompt":"Write 250–400 words comparing (1) level flight, (2) a climb that loses speed, and (3) a steep banked turn. Identify force balances, energy changes, angle-of-attack concerns, and at least three things the current simulator cannot determine. Include a diagram description."},{"module":1,"title":"Textbook Unit 4 — Flight instruments and evidence-based interpretation","level":1,"sections":[["How measurements become cockpit information","A flight instrument is the final stage of a measurement chain. A physical phenomenon is sensed, converted into a signal, sometimes filtered or corrected, and presented to the pilot. Airspeed indicators commonly use pitot and static pressure; barometric altitude uses static pressure and an altimeter setting. Electronic flight displays may integrate several sensors and computers. Understanding the origin of each number is essential because an instrument may display a plausible value even when its source is wrong.","Measurement accuracy, precision and update rate are different properties. A precise-looking altitude readout may be biased by an incorrect pressure setting. A stable heading display may rely on an imperfect magnetic reference. The student should ask what each number represents, which assumptions are embedded in the reading, and what independent evidence can corroborate it."],["Indicated, true and ground-relative quantities","Indicated airspeed is related to sensed dynamic pressure, whereas true airspeed describes aircraft movement through the surrounding air. Groundspeed reflects velocity relative to Earth's surface, including wind. These values can differ substantially and should not be used interchangeably. Similarly, pressure altitude, indicated altitude and geometric height above terrain answer different questions and can carry different uncertainties.","When atmospheric density changes, a given indicated airspeed generally corresponds to a different true airspeed. A GPS-derived ground speed cannot independently determine indicated airspeed without knowing the wind vector. Interpreting the instruments as a coherent system requires matching quantities, units and frames of reference rather than comparing superficially similar numbers."],["Attitude, heading and vertical-motion indications","An attitude indicator represents aircraft orientation, typically pitch and bank, as estimated by gyroscopic or electronic inertial equipment. Heading may use magnetic sensing or integrated reference systems. Vertical-speed indications commonly derive from changes in pressure or estimated vertical motion; they may lag or become unreliable under certain conditions. Knowing the sensor pathways helps students recognize how distinct displayed errors can share one cause.","An apparent disagreement need not indicate an emergency in every context. Different sensors may measure slightly different physical quantities or update at different rates. It is still important to distinguish normal measurement limitations from actual equipment anomalies. Aircraft-specific operational decisions belong to approved manuals and professional flight training."],["Independent cross-checks and disciplined reasoning","Good diagnostic reasoning starts with observations and multiple hypotheses. If two displays agree because they use a shared air-data computer, their agreement is weaker evidence than two independent measurement systems. A display powered by a separate bus can still share the same pressure sensor; redundancy should be traced through the full dependency chain.","A classroom analysis should document indication, source, likely failure paths, independent corroboration and what remains unknown. The goal is not to memorize one universal failure response, but to develop a foundation for learning aircraft-specific procedures with a qualified instructor."]],"worked":"A fictional navigation display shows a ground speed of 105 kt and an airspeed display shows 90 kt. The 15-kt difference is not, by itself, evidence of faulty instruments: wind and measurement frames must be considered. Draw three possible wind vectors and explain what additional data would distinguish them.","optionalPrompt":"Sketch a sensor-to-display dependency diagram for airspeed, altitude, attitude and heading. Identify at least one shared failure pathway.","questions":[["Why might two identical cockpit displays show the same incorrect information?",["They depend on a shared sensor or processor","Every display independently measures every variable","Groundspeed and airspeed must always match"],0,"Redundant-looking displays may share measurement dependencies."]]},{"module":2,"title":"Textbook Unit 2 — Aircraft systems, redundancy and failure reasoning","level":1,"sections":[["System boundaries and interdependence","An airplane is a system of systems. Propulsion, fuel delivery, electrical generation, avionics, environmental control and flight controls have their own components but share physical and operational dependencies. Engineers describe a system by its inputs, outputs, interfaces and failure states before interpreting what a warning might mean. A voltage indication, for example, is not a diagnosis of alternator failure unless the sensor, bus topology, loading and battery behavior are understood.","Architecture varies significantly between piston airplanes, turbine aircraft and electrically powered designs. A magneto-based ignition arrangement can differ markedly from an ignition system relying on electrical power. Generic statements such as 'electrical failure stops the engine' are therefore not universally true. Technical study should identify the applicable aircraft model and use its actual documentation."],["Fuel and propulsion as a reliability chain","Fuel must be suitable, available, delivered and correctly managed for the specific propulsion system. A complete analysis may involve tanks, vents, selectors, pumps, filters, plumbing and engine controls. Mechanical or electronic instrumentation can show quantity and pressure, but each measurement has limitations. A quantity sensor does not prove that an engine can receive usable fuel, just as an indicated pressure does not establish that fuel quality is acceptable.","Propulsion performance also depends on air density, temperatures, mechanical condition and control configuration. When constructing a classroom failure tree, begin with an observed symptom, identify several plausible upstream causes, then list measurements that could differentiate them. In real aircraft, abnormal procedures must come from approved documentation and qualified instruction."],["Electrical capacity and stored energy","Power is energy per unit time. In many aircraft electrical systems, the useful engineering approximation is P = V × I, where volts multiplied by amperes gives watts. If a device draws 5 amperes at 24 volts, nominal electrical power is 120 watts. Battery capacity is frequently expressed in amp-hours, but usable operating duration also depends on voltage, discharge rate, battery chemistry, temperature and condition.","It is tempting to divide a battery label by current draw and announce an endurance value. This can be dangerous as an operating assumption because equipment may require a minimum voltage and some battery capacity cannot be safely used. A credible estimate needs component specifications, variable loads, conservative reserves and independent verification."],["Redundancy, common causes and human factors","Two pieces of equipment may look redundant while still sharing one power bus, sensor, cooling loop or software source. True resilience requires examination of the pathways by which a single failure could disable multiple functions. Engineers use block diagrams and failure-mode analysis to find those common points and understand how the remaining functions change.","Human interpretation is another part of the system. A crew that incorrectly assumes two displays are independent may gain false confidence from matching numbers. A good debrief separates direct observation, likely hypotheses, evidence gaps and real-world operational limits. This academy teaches reasoning, not aircraft-specific emergency troubleshooting instructions."]],"worked":"A fictional avionics device uses 5 A on a nominal 24 V system, so P ≈ 120 W. Three such devices together would represent about 360 W if the assumed voltage and currents remained constant. This arithmetic does not determine safe battery endurance or the power distribution behavior of a real aircraft.","optionalPrompt":"Sketch the dependency chain between alternator, battery, distribution bus, display and shared sensor. Describe how a common failure might mislead two displays, and what additional independent evidence would clarify the problem.","questions":[["What is a common-mode failure?",["One fault affecting multiple supposedly independent functions","A guarantee that a backup works","An unrelated visual design problem"],0,"Shared dependencies can defeat apparent redundancy."]]},{"module":3,"title":"Textbook Unit 5 — Navigation, weather and flight planning","level":1,"sections":[["Navigation as an estimate","Flight navigation links position, time, velocity and the surrounding environment. A route is a series of planned locations, but actual ground track depends on air-relative motion, wind and decisions made along the way. A position estimate has uncertainty; GPS, charts, radio navigation and pilot observations have their own limitations and failure modes.","A useful plan states departure conditions, route constraints, waypoint sequence, expected progress, available alternatives and criteria for revising decisions. Aeronautical charts communicate airspace and terrain information but may change; current authoritative data are essential for actual flight."],["Wind vectors and time estimates","Velocity is a vector, so an aircraft's ground velocity is the sum of its velocity relative to air and the motion of that air. A direct headwind can be subtracted from route-aligned true airspeed in a simplified model. Crosswinds require a wind triangle and can shift heading away from desired track. Wind direction conventions and units must be handled consistently.","Time estimation follows distance divided by ground speed only when a meaningful average ground speed can be assumed. Speed changes, routing constraints, weather deviations and climb or descent segments create uncertainty. Flight-planning calculations should explicitly separate arithmetic examples from real regulatory fuel and reserve requirements."],["Weather as dynamic risk information","Visibility, cloud, wind, turbulence, icing and convective hazards can affect flight safety in very different ways. A forecast is a probabilistic representation, not a guarantee for the entire flight. Observations may be local, dated or incomplete. Understanding weather means combining current reports, forecasts, terrain effects and the aircraft's capabilities.","Hazard management must be aircraft- and pilot-specific. A technique suitable for one airplane or qualified crew may not be appropriate for another. In training, students can compare alternatives such as delay, cancellation and route changes without treating a classroom outcome as operational approval."],["Decision gates and alternatives","A route should include moments at which updated evidence is compared with predefined constraints. A diversion or cancellation is not a failed learning outcome when it preserves safety. This is especially important when schedule pressure or personal investment encourages an unjustified continuation.","A strong debrief asks which information was available at the decision point and which facts were only learned afterward. This distinction limits hindsight bias and improves future planning."]],"worked":"A conceptual airplane travels at 110 kt true airspeed directly along its planned track into a 15-kt headwind. Approximate groundspeed is 95 kt. For 190 nautical miles, a simplified estimate is 190/95 = 2 hours, excluding climb, descent, routing and reserves. Explain why this cannot be used as a real-world flight-release calculation.","optionalPrompt":"Plan an imaginary three-leg route and identify assumptions, wind uncertainty, weather decision gates and alternatives.","questions":[["What determines the ground velocity of an aircraft?",["Air-relative velocity plus wind as vectors","Indicated airspeed alone","The aircraft's compass color"],0,"Ground velocity is the vector sum of aircraft velocity through air and wind."]]},{"module":4,"title":"Textbook Unit 6 — Approach, landing and energy management","level":1,"sections":[["Approach energy and stability","Approach management requires coordinating speed, height, alignment, configuration and descent path. An airplane can be too fast, too high or poorly aligned even while appearing visually close to the runway. Energy includes both kinetic energy associated with motion and gravitational potential energy associated with altitude. A change in one may affect the other.","A stable approach is evaluated using aircraft-specific published criteria. This academic unit discusses the reasoning, not control commands for a real landing. No universal vertical-speed or airspeed number certifies a safe approach across all aircraft types."],["Runway geometry and environmental constraints","A landing must account for usable runway length, surface condition, slope, wind and obstacle clearance. A smooth touchdown far beyond the intended touchdown zone can leave inadequate stopping distance. A small vertical speed cannot compensate for poor runway alignment or unfavorable braking conditions.","Water, contamination and runway slope change performance. Wind gusts and crosswinds influence lateral motion and control demands. Aircraft manuals and authorized operating procedures provide specific limitations and methods that cannot be recreated from a simplified game score."],["Judgment, discontinuation and alternatives","The decision to continue an approach should be governed by verifiable criteria rather than determination to complete a landing. If a hypothetical simulation shows deteriorating alignment or excess energy, the learning question is whether the pilot recognizes the changed situation and knows a safer alternative must be considered.","In real aviation, go-around decisions and execution require training and aircraft-specific procedures. The academy can teach the judgment framework without asserting that its simulator models the full maneuver accurately."],["Debriefing a simulated landing","After a simulated approach, separate observable variables from inferred safety: indicated speed, track, touchdown position, runway remaining and environmental conditions. Some variables may not be modeled or may be approximated. A useful learning record includes assumptions and limitations alongside the score.","A sophisticated performance system evaluates patterns rather than one number. A student who recognizes an unstable approach and chooses an appropriate simulated alternative may demonstrate more understanding than one who receives an artificially favorable touchdown grade."]],"worked":"A simulated runway has 1,600 m available. One touchdown occurs 300 m from the threshold, another 1,100 m from it; the remaining straight-line distances are 1,300 m and 500 m respectively. These numbers do not determine real stopping performance, but they show why touchdown location matters alongside vertical speed.","optionalPrompt":"Describe a five-factor approach assessment and identify what the educational simulator cannot verify.","questions":[["Which additional variable matters even when touchdown is smooth?",["Remaining usable runway","Only the score animation","The color of the runway lights"],0,"Stopping distance, alignment, conditions and remaining runway remain important."]]},{"module":5,"title":"Textbook Unit 3 — Aviation judgment and safety systems","level":1,"sections":[["Risk as a decision process","Safety management is more than a list of hazards. It asks what can go wrong, how serious the consequence would be, how likely it is under given conditions, and which protective measures might reduce exposure. A risk assessment should distinguish known conditions from guesses; a forecast is evidence with uncertainty rather than a guarantee. A mitigation that is adequate for one aircraft, crew or day may be inadequate for another.","A good safety process establishes limitations before an activity starts. New information should trigger reassessment rather than defend an old plan. The goal is not to make every activity possible; declining or delaying an operation may be the safest and most professionally appropriate decision."],["Human factors and cognitive biases","Confirmation bias occurs when a person seeks or interprets evidence mainly to support an existing belief. Plan-continuation bias appears when a team persists with an objective despite accumulating reasons to reconsider. Schedule pressure, fatigue, workload and social dynamics make these biases more difficult to notice. An honest operating culture allows people to report uncertainty or challenge assumptions.","A simple countermeasure is to ask what observation would change the decision. A pre-agreed threshold can reduce arguments motivated by sunk costs. Crew resource management relies on communication, shared situation awareness and effective use of available resources; it is not merely a hierarchy of instructions."],["Structured briefings and debriefings","A briefing identifies objectives, known hazards, assumptions, planned checks, responsibilities and conditions that would trigger a change of plan. It makes information visible to everyone involved and creates a record for later learning. A debrief then separates facts from interpretations and considers alternative explanations for both success and failure.","A high simulator score should not end the investigation. An apparently successful landing may omit runway condition, aircraft-specific limits or realism in the aerodynamic model. Learning improves when the student asks why an outcome occurred and which hidden assumptions could change it."],["Learning from safety events","An incident analysis can use a timeline of decisions, indications and environmental conditions. It should avoid hindsight bias: knowledge gained after an event may not have been available to participants at the time. Multiple contributing factors may be present even when the last visible action receives most attention.","The most useful conclusion proposes changes that could be evaluated—better information, clearer limits, independent checks or improved training. Professional aviation instruction and regulatory standards remain necessary for real-world competency, regardless of how complete an online academy becomes."]],"worked":"A fictional training event was booked weeks in advance. The updated forecast conflicts with a pre-established personal minimum. Compare three options—continue, delay or cancel—using current hazards and acceptable limits. The money already spent is unrecoverable and does not make continuation safer.","optionalPrompt":"Draft a short briefing template with objective, evidence, major uncertainty, stop criteria and debrief questions. It is an optional reflection, not a graded assignment.","questions":[["Which response best counters plan-continuation bias?",["Reevaluate using current hazards and pre-agreed limits","Continue because the event was paid for","Ignore alternative interpretations"],0,"Risk should be reassessed based on current conditions, not sunk costs."]]},{"module":0,"title":"Level 2 — Stability, load factor and the flight envelope","level":2,"sections":[["Forces during maneuvering","Level flight is a useful baseline but airplanes rarely remain in one unchanging equilibrium. When an aircraft banks while maintaining a constant-altitude coordinated turn, the lift vector tilts: only its vertical component supports weight. That component must still approximately balance weight, so total lift must rise. This is why maneuvering can increase aerodynamic demand even when the aircraft seems to be holding altitude.","The idealized relationship for a level coordinated turn is load factor n = 1/cos(bank angle). At 45 degrees bank, cos(45°) is about 0.707 and load factor is about 1.41 g. This formula omits maneuver transients and is not a procedure for conducting turns. It illustrates that the structure and wing encounter changing demands."],["Stall margins in turning flight","The maximum usable lift coefficient depends on angle of attack and configuration. Higher load factor requires greater lift at similar air density and speed. As a result, a simplified stall speed for a particular configuration scales approximately with the square root of load factor: Vs,new ≈ Vs,reference × √n. This relationship helps explain why an aircraft may reach its critical angle of attack at a higher indicated speed during a loaded maneuver.","Suppose a classroom reference stall speed is 50 units and n = 1.44. Then an idealized loaded value would be 50 × √1.44 = 60 units. Real aircraft limitations, instrumentation and configuration complicate the picture. The point is to recognize changing margin, not to memorize a universal safe maneuvering speed."],["Static and dynamic stability","Static stability describes the initial tendency of a disturbed aircraft to return toward its equilibrium. Dynamic stability describes how the aircraft actually behaves over time: oscillations may decay, persist or grow. Stability is influenced by wing and tail arrangement, center of gravity, aerodynamic derivatives and flight condition.","An airplane can be statically stable yet have poorly damped oscillations. Stability also differs by pitch, roll and yaw; it is not one all-or-nothing attribute. Engineers examine the aircraft's response to small disturbances and the coupling between axes rather than judge stability from a single cockpit indication."],["Center of gravity and loading","Changing the center of gravity affects control authority, trim requirements and handling characteristics. Loading constraints involve both total weight and distribution; a number under maximum gross weight does not automatically mean the aircraft is within its approved center-of-gravity envelope.","A classroom exercise can show how a weighted-average location is determined using moments. Operational weight and balance calculations, however, must use the actual aircraft's approved data and loading procedures, not hypothetical examples from this academy."],["The envelope is a boundary, not a goal","A flight envelope summarizes combinations of conditions in which an aircraft is intended to operate. Structural load limits, airspeed restrictions and aerodynamic behavior interact. An aircraft simulator that models only velocity and pitch angle cannot guarantee that it reproduces those limits.","A sound safety decision preserves margin rather than trying to operate at the exact numerical boundary. The training objective is to interpret limits and uncertainty, then seek qualified flight instruction for physical aircraft handling."]],"worked":"For a simplified level coordinated turn at a 60° bank, n=1/cos60°=2. If an illustrative reference stall speed were 50 kt, the simplified loaded stall speed would be 50√2≈70.7 kt at identical configuration and other assumptions. These calculations do not authorize a real maneuver or substitute for aircraft-approved operating limits.","optionalPrompt":"Compare a 30° and 45° bank using n=1/cosφ. Explain why load factor, stall margin and structural limits need to be considered together.","questions":[["At greater load factor, a simplified stall-speed estimate generally:",["Increases as approximately the square root of load factor","Decreases to zero","Stays unchanged under every condition"],0,"Higher load factor increases lift demand and typically raises the stall speed for a given configuration."]]},{"module":1,"title":"Level 2 — Air-data integrity and sensor fault hypotheses","level":2,"sections":[["Pressure sensing is a chain","Pressure instruments do not directly observe all the quantities shown on the display. A static system senses ambient pressure while a pitot system senses total pressure under appropriate airflow conditions. Their difference can be interpreted as dynamic pressure, which relates to indicated airspeed. Blockage, leaks, instrument processing, configuration and pressure-setting errors may affect readings differently.","In an integrated glass cockpit, several displayed values might originate from one air-data computer. A second display does not necessarily create a second independent pressure source. Students should trace the physical port, plumbing, sensing element, computation, transmission and display for each value."],["Disagreement without premature diagnosis","Imagine that indicated airspeed remains steady while GPS groundspeed changes. A change in wind is one potential explanation; sensor errors or maneuvering can also be relevant. Because groundspeed and airspeed represent different reference frames, disagreement alone cannot confirm an air-data fault.","A robust review begins with a timeline of observed values and which quantities are comparable. It explicitly lists independent measurements, uncertainties and plausible hypotheses. The objective is to avoid turning a symptom into a confident diagnosis before evidence supports it."],["Latency and data filtering","A sensor may sample frequently while the cockpit displays a filtered or delayed estimate. A sudden change in aircraft motion can make two valid instruments appear momentarily inconsistent. Conversely, filtering may hide a rapidly changing fault. Delay must be considered when reconstructing event sequences.","A display with more digits is not necessarily more accurate. Resolution, bias, noise, time alignment and calibration are separate characteristics. Teaching students to use the correct terms creates the foundation for more advanced avionics and maintenance study."],["Common causes and redundancy","Independent information sources add value only if their failure pathways are sufficiently separate. Two processors connected to the same static source share that source's vulnerability. A separate energy supply is useful but does not remove common software, antenna or sensor faults.","Dependency diagrams help distinguish a duplicated screen from duplicated measurement. The exercise is analytical: it does not provide an in-flight troubleshooting checklist. Actual responses must follow the aircraft's approved procedures and qualified instruction."]],"worked":"A navigation display reports 110 kt groundspeed while an air-data display reports 95 kt true airspeed. A direct 15 kt tailwind could account for these classroom numbers, though wind direction, track, instrument definitions and uncertainties must be checked. The difference does not prove either reading is erroneous.","optionalPrompt":"Make a five-column chart for altitude, airspeed, heading, attitude and groundspeed: quantity, physical sensing method, processing dependency, possible bias and independent comparison.","questions":[["When two screens use one sensor, their agreement is:",["Not independent confirmation of sensor accuracy","Proof that the sensor is always correct","Evidence that data latency is zero"],0,"Both displays may repeat the same faulty measurement."]]},{"module":3,"title":"Level 2 — Weather systems, uncertainty and route alternatives","level":2,"sections":[["Weather information has scope","A weather observation describes a particular place and time. Forecasts describe anticipated conditions and include uncertainty. Conditions at an airport, along an airway and near elevated terrain may differ substantially. Students must distinguish observed data, modeled forecasts and reports of hazards that may not be uniformly distributed.","Meteorological variables interact. Temperature and moisture affect clouds and visibility; wind changes with height and terrain; frontal boundaries and convective processes can produce rapidly changing hazards. A useful interpretation starts with context and never assumes one report represents an entire region."],["Wind triangles in two dimensions","When wind is not directly aligned with a desired ground track, the pilot must account for a cross-track component. The relationship is vector addition: aircraft air-relative velocity plus wind equals ground-relative velocity. A useful conceptual diagram has the desired course, aircraft heading and wind vector as distinct arrows.","This geometry explains why an airplane can point away from its ground track and still progress along the desired route. Real navigation must also honor aircraft-specific operating procedures, airspace constraints and weather limitations. A wind triangle is a mathematical model, not authorization to fly in difficult conditions."],["Information quality and changing decisions","A route developed in the morning might no longer be reasonable after a later weather update. The planning process should set conditions for reassessment and know which data sources are authoritative and current. A new forecast can change a time estimate, reserve needs or the acceptability of the entire activity.","Good planning rewards the decision to delay, reroute or cancel when uncertainty or hazard increases. It also records which information was available at each decision point rather than judging everything through hindsight."],["Risk communication","A brief report should distinguish known facts from interpretations and say which critical questions remain unresolved. Terms such as 'likely,' 'possible' and 'not yet observed' carry different meanings. A leader who recognizes uncertainty can make better choices than one who creates false confidence.","Flight Academy uses weather scenarios for education only. Real flight planning and decisions require current authoritative aviation weather products, aircraft-specific limits and professional instruction."]],"worked":"A hypothetical 180-nautical-mile route at 120 kt groundspeed takes 1.5 hours in a constant-speed model. If revised winds reduce groundspeed to 90 kt, the same nominal leg takes 2 hours. The extra 30 minutes illustrates why changing weather affects planning, though real fuel and reserve calculations are more complex.","optionalPrompt":"Compare two fictitious route scenarios using forecast age, terrain exposure, wind uncertainty and a decision to divert. Explain which unknown matters most.","questions":[["Why should a route be reevaluated when a forecast changes?",["Expected hazards and travel time can change","Earlier planning guarantees future conditions","Wind never affects ground speed"],0,"Weather updates can change risk, duration and available alternatives."]]}],"survival":[{"module":0,"title":"Textbook Unit 1 — Land navigation and off-grid route intelligence","level":1,"objectives":["Interpret topographic contour patterns and map scale","Separate true, grid and magnetic north without a universal conversion shortcut","Use reliable checkpoints, handrails, catching features and route alternatives","Quantify distance-estimation uncertainty","Apply lost-person decision principles in safe field exercises"],"sections":[["Maps as models of terrain","Topographic maps represent elevation through contour lines, where all points on one contour share an elevation. The contour interval is the vertical difference between adjacent contour lines, not the ground distance between them. Widely spaced contours typically indicate gentle slopes; crowded lines signal more rapid elevation change. Ridges, valleys, spurs and saddles can be recognized through repeating contour patterns. Interpreting them is more reliable when checked against visible landmarks.","Maps are snapshots of changing terrain and infrastructure. Trails may be rerouted, bridges damaged, property boundaries revised and vegetation altered. A route drawn on an old map is not proof of current access or passability. Study the legend, scale, map date, coordinate datum, north reference, trail status and local restrictions before field use. Always tell another person where you intend to go and when you expect to return."],["Scale and coordinate discipline","A map with scale 1:25,000 means 1 unit on the map represents 25,000 of the same units on the ground in horizontal plan view. Four centimeters therefore represents 100,000 centimeters, or one kilometer. That straight-line horizontal distance is not necessarily the distance walked. Switchbacks, obstacles, elevation, conditions and lawful access can force a longer path. Time estimates based on horizontal distance are hypotheses that need adjustment.","Coordinates must be represented with their system and datum. Latitude and longitude use angular references; Universal Transverse Mercator uses zones and metric eastings and northings. A UTM coordinate without its zone and datum can be ambiguous. Before exchanging coordinates with a responder, use the exact format they request and verify the displayed position. More digits are not necessarily more accuracy when the underlying measurement is uncertain."],["Bearings and the three norths","True north points toward Earth's geographic north pole. Magnetic north is the direction indicated by Earth's local magnetic field; grid north follows the map's chosen rectangular grid. The angles between these references can vary with place and time. Magnetic declination is not a fixed adjustment that is always added or always subtracted. The correct conversion requires the selected map reference, a current local declination and a carefully checked diagram.","Nearby vehicles, electrical equipment and large metal objects may affect compass readings. A prudent field exercise begins by checking for interference in an open safe area, orienting the map to known terrain and verifying that a planned bearing matches a visible landmark. Never follow a line into a cliff, flood channel, restricted area or unsafe crossing solely because the bearing is mathematically correct."],["Route architecture and error control","A navigable route contains a known start, a destination, intermediate checkpoints and options for changing course. A handrail is a clearly identifiable linear terrain or built feature that helps you stay oriented. An attack point is an easy-to-recognize landmark near the intended objective. A catching feature beyond the destination reveals that you may have overshot. Choose features that are distinctive in the relevant terrain and season, and use other evidence to confirm them.","Dead reckoning advances a position estimate using direction and distance. Both inputs contain errors: pacing changes with load, terrain, fatigue and visibility; bearings can be misread or transferred using the wrong north reference. With each unverified segment, uncertainty usually accumulates. Good navigators compare estimated position to terrain association, trail signs and other independent sources, rather than continuing as though the estimated point were exact."],["Decision-making when disoriented","If an individual or group is unsure of its location, stopping is often the best first response when the current location is safe. Assess injuries, weather, exposure, dwindling daylight and communications. Identify the last positively known point and compare all available location clues. Seek emergency assistance when appropriate and communicate coordinates, landmarks, people affected and hazards in clear terms. Preserve battery power and avoid speculative travel that makes search-and-rescue more difficult.","Some places are unsafe to remain in, such as active flood zones or unstable slopes. The correct response may then involve moving to a clearly safer nearby location while minimizing navigation risk. The lesson is not 'always stay' or 'always move'; it is to make an evidence-based safety decision, and to avoid crossing dangerous terrain or relying solely on an uncertain heading."],["Supervised practical navigation design","Begin with a safe park or maintained trail network where exit routes are clear, conditions are mild and travel is authorized. Carry a current map, compass, charged phone, basic supplies and a communication plan. Set short known checkpoints and compare your estimated positions against fixed signs or identifiable landmarks. Practice identifying terrain features, estimating distances and describing uncertainty. Never intentionally become lost, practice alone in remote terrain or use restricted land.","A useful debrief compares planned bearing and distance with the observed route, identifies deviations and asks which independent observations resolved ambiguity. The goal is to build a repeatable, accountable decision process before considering more complex off-grid scenarios."]],"worked":"Worked map-scale problem: on a 1:25,000 topographic map, a proposed direct line is 6 cm, representing 1.5 km horizontally. A maintained trail is 9 cm, representing 2.25 km, before terrain corrections. Explain why the longer maintained trail may nevertheless be preferable. In a separate exercise, suppose two hypothetical legs have measured distances of 400 m and 600 m. Their 1,000 m sum is an estimate, not a guarantee of actual walking distance.","questions":[["At 1:25,000 map scale, 6 cm represents how much horizontal distance?",["1.5 km","15 km","150 m"],0,"6 × 25,000 cm = 150,000 cm = 1.5 km."]],"optionalPrompt":"Produce a 250–400-word tabletop navigation plan: map datum and scale, known starting landmark, two checkpoints, handrail, catching feature, hazard avoidance, lawful access, communication plan, turnaround conditions and route-recovery strategy. Do not create instructions to trespass or cross hazards."},{"module":1,"title":"Textbook Unit 4 — Medical preparedness and communication continuity","level":1,"sections":[["Priorities before equipment","Emergency medical readiness begins with knowing the household's actual health dependencies: medications, medical devices, mobility support, transport, emergency contacts and access to professional care. Equipment is useful only when it matches those requirements. A kit cannot replace a clinician's advice, medication supply continuity or hands-on first-aid training.","Preparedness planning should distinguish common first-aid supplies from equipment requiring specialized use. A person who has completed an online lesson should not assume competence in procedures requiring supervision. Recognizing urgent danger signs and seeking emergency help are central skills."],["Medication and power dependencies","Some medications need controlled storage conditions and some equipment needs reliable electricity. Requirements vary by model and medicine. A power outage plan needs device-specific ratings, manufacturer instructions, approved alternatives and consultation with a pharmacist or clinician. Improvised refrigeration or unverified replacement treatments may create additional harm.","A household should maintain a current medication list and accessible contacts while protecting sensitive health information. When conditions change, the response should reflect the individual's actual needs rather than a generic preparedness checklist."],["Communicating when networks fail","Cellular, internet and electrical services can be disrupted together. Backup communication may include battery-powered radio reception of official advisories, a paper contact list and agreed meeting locations. No method offers guaranteed coverage or reliability. A plan should identify how people will check in, preserve batteries and communicate a verified location.","A situation report should state time, location, observable hazards, affected people, immediate needs and information that is uncertain. Short factual reporting is more useful than dramatic speculation, especially when communications capacity is limited."],["Coordination and inclusion","Emergency preparedness should explicitly include children, people with disabilities, older adults and those who need specialized equipment. Evacuation transport, shelter accessibility, interpretation and continuity of care can be deciding factors long before supplies run out.","A community plan is strongest when people volunteer meaningful roles and have clear privacy boundaries. Where official support remains available, it should be included rather than disregarded."]],"worked":"A hypothetical medical device draws 60 W for eight hours. Its ideal energy demand is 480 Wh, before converter losses and other loads. This arithmetic does not establish what backup equipment is medically appropriate; device-specific operating requirements and professional guidance are necessary.","optionalPrompt":"Review a fictional household's medical and communication dependencies, including what must be verified with a pharmacist or clinician.","questions":[["What is the most reliable basis for planning medication storage?",["Medication-specific manufacturer and professional instructions","One temperature assumption for every medicine","An unrelated consumer product review"],0,"Storage and continuity requirements vary by medication and individual."]]},{"module":2,"title":"Textbook Unit 2 — The first 180 days of prolonged societal disruption","level":1,"sections":[["Understanding what has actually failed","Civilization collapse is a dramatic label, but real disruptions have different causes, geographic extent and recovery paths. A local grid failure, a flood that damages water treatment, and a prolonged transport interruption can produce different problems even when they look similarly disruptive to households. Plans should begin with reliable observations: whether clean water is available, whether emergency services are operating, whether transport routes are open, and whether residents can obtain medicines.","A scenario matrix can distinguish short-term, regional and longer multi-system disruptions. Each scenario should specify evidence that would justify moving to the next plan. Assuming that every institution has ceased functioning can cause unnecessarily risky decisions. Maintaining access to official assistance and community resources is part of preparedness, not a contradiction of independence."],["Hours zero through seventy-two","The first three days prioritize immediate safety, hazard-specific shelter or evacuation, dependable information, required medicines, accessible communication and safe drinking water. Households should inspect risks before consuming stored resources or traveling. A battery-powered radio, written contact list and agreed meeting points can help when digital communication is unreliable, although reception and power limitations remain.","The first decision should often be whether the current building is safe for the specific threat. Flooding, smoke, damaged structures and extreme indoor temperatures can make staying dangerous. Conversely, travel without a verified safe route can be worse than sheltering. Official emergency instructions and credible current hazard observations should guide the choice."],["Days four through thirty","A longer disruption changes the central problem from immediate supplies to operational continuity. Water storage has to remain clean; sanitation systems may require alternative arrangements; perishable food needs safe handling; and medication access may become an urgent constraint. The same person who can endure a short inconvenience may be at serious risk if a life-supporting device or temperature-sensitive medicine cannot be maintained.","Inventory management should record starting amounts, reasonable daily use assumptions, damage, resupply opportunities and uncertainty. Treat the weakest essential dependency as the limiting factor. A household with weeks of shelf-stable food but no potable water should not claim weeks of self-sufficiency."],["Days thirty through ninety","Beyond a month, maintenance, sanitation and replenishment demand planning. Fuel and replacement parts may be difficult to obtain. Gardens take time to establish and may not provide enough calories; clean water is needed for both people and food preparation. Skills in safe repair, recordkeeping, first aid awareness and communication become increasingly important, but specialized electrical, medical or structural work must not be improvised beyond competence.","Voluntary neighborhood cooperation can lower the burden on any one household. People may bring different resources and skills, yet fair agreements, privacy, consent and realistic expectations are necessary. A sustainable plan protects vulnerable people and creates a process for handling disputes without escalation."],["Days ninety through one hundred eighty","A six-month scenario tests whether systems can be repeated, not merely started. Water supplies and treatment materials require monitoring, food storage and production vary with the season, equipment wears out and people need continuing healthcare. Mental strain and fatigue are practical operational concerns; humane schedules, rest and shared responsibilities can prevent poor decisions.","Recovery must also be part of the plan. Track verified service restoration, transportation options and health-authority instructions. Retaining flexibility helps a household reintegrate with repaired infrastructure instead of remaining isolated by assumption. No model can guarantee that a community can maintain all essential services indefinitely."],["Ethics and limits of collapse planning","Preparedness should not presume that other people are threats or that resources should be seized. Communities typically function better when residents share accurate information, protect water and sanitation, coordinate care, respect property and de-escalate disputes. Protection of children, older adults and disabled residents is integral rather than optional.","An evidence-based academy explicitly separates plausible emergencies from speculative predictions. It teaches how to analyze dependencies, protect health and preserve social cooperation. It does not promise that one kit or survival strategy can solve every infrastructure failure."]],"worked":"Consider a fictional town that loses power for 30 days but retains functioning road access and neighboring hospitals. Divide household planning into first 72 hours, days 4–30 and recovery. Explain why maintaining access to functioning outside services is preferable to assuming all institutions have failed. Make no unsupported claims about specific survival-duration guarantees.","optionalPrompt":"Optionally map water, sanitation, medicine, food, temperature, information and transport for a fictional 180-day scenario. Identify one limiting dependency and one trigger to request outside support.","questions":[["What distinguishes sustainable multi-month preparedness from a stockpile?",["Repeatable safe systems and ongoing maintenance","A one-time purchase without monitoring","Assuming all services fail everywhere simultaneously"],0,"Long-term resilience requires maintenance, health safeguards and adaptable coordination."]]},{"module":3,"title":"Textbook Unit 5 — Food, water and sanitation in extended emergencies","level":1,"sections":[["Water source, contamination and safety","A useful water plan separates collection, transport, storage, treatment and verification. The appearance of a source does not prove its safety. Biological contamination, fuel residues, dissolved chemicals and other hazards can require different responses. Boiling can address many microbial hazards under appropriate guidance but does not reliably remove many chemical contaminants.","Local public-health advisories and verified water-source information should guide action. If chemical contamination is suspected, simply boiling questionable water may be inappropriate. Containers, transfer tools and storage practices can recontaminate treated water."],["Water as a household system","Daily water needs vary with weather, activity, age, health, food preparation and sanitation. Planning is not just a numerical stockpile target; it is a dependency graph showing water requirements, protected sources and failure conditions. A plan should identify what is safe, what is uncertain and when outside assistance is necessary.","Short outages can often be managed with known-safe stored supplies. Longer periods require reliable replenishment and sanitation methods, which may depend on functioning infrastructure, vetted treatment systems and local regulatory guidance."],["Nutrition and food continuity","Food security involves calories, nutrients, storage life, preparation energy, safe temperature and replenishment. Shelf-stable items may be useful but their preparation can require water and fuel. Gardening can diversify diets, yet yields depend on land, season, soil, pest pressure, labor and water; it rarely replaces an entire food supply immediately.","Preservation methods must be food-specific and tested. Incorrectly processed foods can cause severe illness, so improvised canning processes are unsuitable. The academy should teach the distinction between validated storage guidance and untested methods."],["Sanitation and infection prevention","Waste management, handwashing, safe food preparation and clean surfaces become more difficult when water and utilities fail. Poor sanitation can undermine apparent food and water preparedness. Illness prevention often produces larger benefits than acquiring an additional specialty tool.","Plans should account for vulnerable people and seek current public-health instructions. Flood damage and sewage contamination are reasons for added caution; direct contact with contaminated water can create significant risks."]],"worked":"A household estimates 28 days of food but only 6 days of known-safe water. The food supply alone cannot establish 28 days of independent operation. Identify the limiting dependency and list which assumptions might allow safe water replenishment without relying on unverified sources.","optionalPrompt":"Sketch a 30-day resource dependency chart covering potable water, preparation fuel, food, sanitation and health needs.","questions":[["Why is boiling insufficient for some contaminated water?",["It may not remove hazardous chemicals","It always removes all pollutants","It ensures stored water never becomes contaminated"],0,"Biological disinfection and chemical contamination require different approaches."]]},{"module":4,"title":"Textbook Unit 3 — Bug-in, evacuation, and route verification","level":1,"sections":[["Why 'stay or go' is a conditional decision","Sheltering and evacuation are strategies for different threats, not competing ideologies. A home may be suitable during a short electrical outage yet unsafe during structural damage, rapidly rising water, wildfire conditions or extreme temperatures. The decision begins with hazard type, official instructions, health needs, shelter habitability and verified access to a safer location.","A preparedness plan specifies decision triggers before an emergency. Examples include an official evacuation order, a confirmed unsafe structure, or inability to sustain medically essential equipment safely. The plan must remain adaptable because actual conditions may differ from expectations."],["Route reconnaissance without overconfidence","Road maps and trail maps help identify possible routes and terrain constraints, but they are not guarantees of availability. Roads may be closed, bridges damaged and visibility poor. Terrain navigation concepts—waypoints, identifiable landmarks, handrails and alternate paths—can improve route description, but they must be combined with current verified warnings and lawful access.","Do not cross flooded roads or streams to remain on a planned path. A longer confirmed-open route can be safer than a short route with a known hazardous crossing. Travel estimates should include fuel, weather, rest, accessibility needs and the possibility of congestion, without inventing precise travel times."],["Shelter habitability and health","Sheltering safely requires suitable temperature, clean air, potable water, sanitation, essential medicines and reliable updates. A building can remain standing while becoming hazardous for someone dependent on powered medical equipment or climate control. Carbon monoxide from fuel-burning equipment is an especially serious danger when generators or cooking devices are misused.","Household assessments should consider infants, older people, disabled residents, pets and people requiring ongoing medical care. A written contact and support plan can help avoid leaving those needs until the final minutes of a deteriorating emergency."],["Communication, family reunification and documentation","Preparedness includes destination information, out-of-area contacts and agreed check-in procedures. Paper copies of necessary records and contact numbers may help when phones or internet services are unavailable. A short situation report conveys location, time, observed hazard, people affected and immediate needs.","The plan should preserve personal privacy and avoid publishing vulnerable people's details unnecessarily. Safe coordination may depend on trusted neighbors, local authorities or accessible public shelters, not merely a private vehicle or a single mobile phone."],["Practice and debrief","A good practice exercise is low-risk: review maps, identify official evacuation resources, verify emergency contacts and check whether supplies and medication information are current. A tabletop scenario can challenge assumptions without placing anyone in danger. Do not intentionally navigate into hazardous areas to 'test' a route.","After a rehearsal, ask which assumptions failed, what evidence was missing, and whether alternate routes or shelter choices were realistic. Updating the plan is part of success. The aim is sound judgment, not an obligation to bug out."]],"worked":"A fictional flood blocks the shortest 15-km route to a public shelter; an alternate 23-km route has been confirmed open by local authorities. Explain why the second route is favored despite being longer. Identify the conditions that would require rechecking either route and the household-specific factors that affect departure.","optionalPrompt":"Create a tabletop choice tree for an outage that becomes a flood emergency. Include official warnings, safe destinations, communications, accessibility and medical continuity.","questions":[["What overrides a static route plan during evacuation?",["Verified current hazards and official closures","The shortest distance on the map","The route's original planned date"],0,"Real-time safety conditions govern whether a route remains usable."]]},{"module":5,"title":"Textbook Unit 6 — Household resilience and community systems","level":1,"sections":[["Understanding energy demands","Power is measured in watts and energy is power multiplied by time, measured in watt-hours. A household resilience plan should identify which loads truly matter: required medical equipment, safe lighting, communications, refrigeration or temperature management. A small battery might support low-power electronics but not high-demand heating or cooling.","Rated capacity is not identical to usable capacity because discharge limits, conversion losses, temperature and aging affect output. Backup systems require realistic recharge assumptions and safe maintenance, not just optimistic battery-label arithmetic."],["Fuel, carbon monoxide and electrical safety","Fuel-burning generators and appliances can create lethal carbon monoxide hazards. Such devices must not be used indoors or in attached garages, and outdoor placement must follow manufacturer and safety guidance. Electrical connections to household circuits involve shock, fire and backfeed risks and may require qualified installation.","A resilience program should teach hazard recognition and appropriate professional boundaries. A consumer should not attempt improvised wiring based on a generic online lesson."],["Maintenance and resource records","Stored water, medicines, food and batteries can deteriorate or become unusable if not inspected. A maintenance schedule and inventory audit reveal these changes before an emergency. Track dates, conditions, defects, replacement needs and people responsible for upkeep.","System boundaries also matter. A battery without a reliable way to recharge is an expiring resource. A garden without safe water and maintained tools cannot be assumed to provide sustained nutrition."],["Mutual aid and sustainable cooperation","Neighbors may combine transport, communication, accessibility support, professional skills and verified alerts. Agreements should be voluntary, lawful and transparent, with respect for privacy, consent and property. De-escalation and fair resource decisions can reduce the harm created by uncertainty and scarcity.","For multi-month disruption planning, include recovery and reconnection with functioning services. The objective is safer continuity of life, not isolation for its own sake or presuming that normal institutions will never return."]],"worked":"An illustrative device uses 15 W for four hours and a radio uses 5 W for two hours. Combined ideal demand is 15×4 + 5×2 = 70 Wh. A nominal 75 Wh battery may not provide that much usable output because of reserve needs and system losses.","optionalPrompt":"Outline a household maintenance calendar and a voluntary neighborhood information-sharing process.","questions":[["A 15 W device operating for four hours uses:",["60 Wh","19 Wh","15 kWh"],0,"Energy equals power times duration: 15×4=60 Wh."]]},{"module":0,"title":"Level 2 — Advanced terrain association and navigation error","level":2,"sections":[["Topography as a set of constraints","Terrain is not only a collection of map symbols; it shapes water movement, slope exposure, visibility and travel cost. Contour patterns can suggest spurs, reentrants, ridgelines, saddles and depressions. But the map cannot show every fallen tree, temporary flood or new access restriction. A route should be treated as a hypothesis checked against observed conditions.","When comparing route options, evaluate not only straight-line distance but steepness, surface, exposure, stream crossings, authorized access, emergency communication and the group's mobility. A maintained trail may be longer but considerably easier to verify than an unmarked shortcut."],["Grid coordinates and reference discipline","UTM coordinates are metric coordinates tied to a zone and datum, while latitude and longitude use angles. A coordinate with missing zone or ambiguous format can cause a serious positional misunderstanding. When giving a position to emergency responders, use their requested format and verify that the reported data reflect current measurements.","Compasses introduce another reference issue: true, grid and magnetic north are not interchangeable. Local declination changes over space and time. The printed conversion diagram should be interpreted with the map's reference conventions and a current source of local magnetic information, not a universal arithmetic slogan."],["Error propagation across legs","A pace count calibrated on flat ground is not a guarantee of distance on mud, steep slopes or uneven trail. Likewise, small bearing errors produce increasing lateral displacement with travel. For a conceptual example, a 5° error over a straight 1-km leg gives about 1,000 × sin5° ≈ 87 m of lateral difference, if all other conditions are idealized.","Multiple legs compound uncertainty if position is not verified at known checkpoints. A navigational practice should be designed around visible landmarks and safe exits so errors can be detected before becoming dangerous."],["Attack points, handrails and catching features","A recognizable feature near the desired destination can serve as an attack point for a shorter final leg. A line-like feature, such as an authorized signed trail or distinctive ridgeline, may serve as a handrail. A larger unmistakable feature beyond the intended point can reveal an overshoot.","These terms describe planning aids, not guarantees. Streams may shift, trails may close, and visibility may degrade. If a feature does not appear where expected, stop and reconsider the location estimate rather than fitting every observation into the original plan."],["Field verification and ethical boundaries","Navigation training should use approved safe routes with reliable communications and a known return plan. A map-and-compass exercise can compare predictions with a verified map point or GPS track without deliberately getting lost. Avoid crossing private property, active waterways, cliffs or restricted terrain to complete an assignment.","Debrief uncertainty and decision quality, not only whether a destination was reached. Knowing when to turn around and seek assistance is a central navigation skill."]],"worked":"At a 1:25,000 map scale, 8 cm represents 2 km horizontally. If a fictional route's direct bearing error is 5°, an ideal straight 2-km projection gives lateral error ≈2,000 sin5°≈174 m. Actual uncertainty also depends on terrain and repeated position fixes; use these calculations as illustrations, not precise field-location predictions.","optionalPrompt":"Plan a safe hypothetical route with two independent position checks and a clear point at which uncertainty would trigger a return or request for help.","questions":[["Why should route estimates be checked at known landmarks?",["Navigation uncertainty can accumulate across legs","All pace counts are exact","Every old map is current"],0,"Independent checkpoints help detect and reduce accumulated estimation error."]]},{"module":2,"title":"Level 2 — Cascading infrastructure failure and long-duration resilience","level":2,"sections":[["Why infrastructure is interconnected","Electricity may power water pumps, communications networks, fuel distribution and some medical systems. Roads support replenishment of medicines, food and replacement parts. Sanitation depends on water, treatment capacity and safe waste handling. A failure in one system can therefore propagate to others, even if the secondary components were not initially damaged.","Dependency mapping identifies these links without assuming every service will fail simultaneously. A regional power outage with functioning neighboring hospitals poses different options from widespread flooding that isolates roads and contaminates water supplies. Scenarios need explicit duration, geographic reach, service status and uncertainty."],["Risk under compound disruptions","Compound events challenge plans that rely on one fallback. A battery may power a radio, but its ability to recharge can depend on daylight and weather. Stored food may be adequate in calories yet require unavailable cooking fuel or water. A medical device may need electricity and access to replacement supplies.","An effective plan records not just a backup, but the backup's dependencies and limits. Avoid claiming complete independence without verification. Professional health, sanitation and electrical expertise remains necessary for specialized decisions."],["The transition from supplies to routines","The first days reward known-safe stored supplies and clear communication. After several weeks, maintenance schedules, sanitary practices, resource accounting and replenishment become decisive. At longer durations, equipment can degrade, medical requirements persist, and food production depends on climate and seasons.","A daily routine may include verified water access, food safety, hygiene, information checks and care coordination. Work should be shared with consent and adjusted to protect vulnerable people. Sustainable planning acknowledges rest, fatigue and the limits of what a small household can safely maintain."],["Community resilience and recovery","Cooperation allows neighbors to share verified alerts, transport and skills in a lawful and voluntary way. Transparent responsibilities and a process for disagreements can lower the probability that scarcity becomes conflict. A preparedness program should not encourage paranoia, intimidation or disregard for property rights.","Recovery is itself a project: track which services have returned, which supplies are still safe and how households can reconnect to reliable support. Planning to cooperate with restored institutions is more realistic than treating isolation as the goal."],["Testing assumptions responsibly","Tabletop exercises can vary the duration and severity of service interruptions and reveal single points of failure. A credible assessment names where data are unknown and where outside expertise is needed. Do not test survival plans by intentionally using unsafe drinking water, withholding medicine or entering disaster zones.","The core skill is adaptive risk management: identify actual hazards, preserve basic health, reassess conditions and use all safe assistance available."]],"worked":"Consider a household with ten days of shelf-stable food, three days of known-safe water and a battery that runs its essential communication equipment for two days. Without a verified way to renew these supplies, the communication function is the first listed resource to expire. If communication can be safely recharged, the water supply may become the next limiting dependency. This is a dependency analysis, not a predicted survival time.","optionalPrompt":"Build an illustrative 30-, 90- and 180-day resilience dependency map for a small community. Mark which assumptions would need public-health or electrical-professional verification.","questions":[["Why can a secondary service fail even when its equipment was not damaged?",["It depends on another disrupted service","Every system is completely independent","Failures always occur at exactly the same time"],0,"Infrastructure systems depend on one another for energy, transport and materials."]]},{"module":3,"title":"Level 2 — Water treatment limits, food security and sanitation systems","level":2,"sections":[["Source evaluation before treatment","Emergency water planning begins by identifying likely hazards and official advisories. Water that looks clean can contain disease-causing organisms; water near an industrial spill can carry chemicals that boiling does not remove. Some contaminants require specialized testing or treatment that a portable field filter cannot provide.","A method suitable for microbial disinfection should not be presented as universal water purification. Public-health agencies may recommend bottled water or prohibit using a source when contamination is chemical or unknown. The safe response must match the actual warning."],["Storage and cross-contamination","Known-safe water can become unsafe when handled in contaminated containers or mixed with questionable supplies. Clean food-grade storage, proper labeling, protected transfer equipment and routine inspection are important. The time for which water can be stored safely depends on the source, treatment and storage practices.","An extended emergency may require separate planning for drinking, food preparation, personal hygiene and cleaning. Those needs compete for the same resources, so realistic inventory management is more useful than one impressive stockpile number."],["Calories, protein and seasonality","A long-term food plan must consider calories, protein, essential nutrients, preparation energy and shelf life. A vegetable plot can add variety but may not provide enough energy year-round for a household. Yields depend on soil fertility, water, temperatures, plant choice, pests and labor.","Gardening is therefore one component of resilience, not immediate total food independence. Plans should include safe access to outside food systems and local assistance whenever those services remain available."],["Preservation and food safety","A loss of refrigeration can quickly change the safety of perishable food. Heating, drying, fermentation and canning each have hazards and scientifically validated limits. Improvised preservation, especially of low-acid foods, can cause severe foodborne illness.","Use tested guidance from public-health or university extension agencies for any actual preservation method. The academy's calculations and examples do not replace a tested recipe, food thermometer or professional advisory."],["Sanitation as a health system","When sewage systems and water treatment are disrupted, waste handling and hand hygiene become essential to preventing disease. Plans must avoid contaminating water sources and should follow local public-health instructions on temporary sanitation. Floodwater, sewage and chemical spills create additional hazards.","A community can lose more health capacity through preventable infection than through temporary inconvenience. Safe water and waste management therefore belong together in any survival framework."]],"worked":"A hypothetical supply contains 150 liters of known-safe water. At an illustrative household use of 15 liters per day, arithmetic yields ten days. But usage varies with health, heat, cooking and hygiene, and stored water safety must be checked. This does not establish that 150 liters is an adequate emergency supply for a particular household.","optionalPrompt":"Create a decision matrix for a boil-water advisory, a chemical 'do not use' warning and an ordinary temporary supply interruption. Identify which actions require public-health confirmation.","questions":[["Why might boiling a contaminated water source be inappropriate?",["Some hazardous chemicals are not removed by boiling","Boiling eliminates all industrial pollutants","Clear water always needs no treatment"],0,"Biological treatment and chemical contamination need different safeguards."]]}],"space":[{"module":0,"title":"Textbook Unit 4 — Space science, gravity and measurement","level":1,"sections":[["Observing the universe","Space science studies objects and processes using electromagnetic radiation, particles, gravitational effects and direct measurements from spacecraft. Telescopes and sensors collect data, but their sensitivity, wavelength ranges and calibration determine what they can infer. Apparent brightness is not identical to intrinsic luminosity because distance and intervening material affect the measured signal.","Scientists compare observations with physical models. A model should make testable predictions and clearly distinguish measurement uncertainty from disagreement with the theory."],["Gravity and free fall","Gravitational forces influence planetary motion and the motion of satellites. Objects in orbit do not float because gravity disappears; they share a free-fall trajectory with the spacecraft. In a circular two-body approximation, gravitational acceleration supplies the inward acceleration required to curve the motion.","Real trajectories depart from the simplest model because of atmospheric drag, nonuniform planetary gravity and other bodies. A basic model can be useful for reasoning as long as its limitations are stated."],["Radiation and the space environment","The space environment includes charged particles, ultraviolet radiation, thermal extremes and, in some orbits, atmospheric drag. Radiation can affect electronics and human health; engineering solutions vary with mission duration, orbit, shielding, mission objectives and acceptable risk.","The absence of air also changes heat exchange and propulsion assumptions. Spacecraft must reject much of their waste heat through radiation, while mechanical design accounts for vacuum exposure and thermal cycling."],["Units, evidence and uncertainty","Measurements require units, timestamps, reference frames and error estimates. Mixing kilometers with meters or interpreting a ground-fixed direction as inertial can produce large mistakes. Scientific conclusions are only as reliable as their calibration and data quality.","Repeated observations, independent sensors and model comparisons make explanations stronger. Students should learn to state what evidence supports a conclusion and what remains uncertain."]],"worked":"If a hypothetical object travels 12,000 km in two hours at constant speed, the average speed is 6,000 km/h, or about 1.67 km/s. This arithmetic does not describe its orbital state without position, direction and reference frame.","optionalPrompt":"Write a short comparison of a scientific observation, a physical model and an engineering prediction.","questions":[["Why can objects inside an orbiting spacecraft appear weightless?",["They share the spacecraft's free-fall motion","Gravity is always zero above the atmosphere","They are pushed upward by air"],0,"Orbital occupants and spacecraft follow approximately the same freely falling trajectory."]]},{"module":1,"title":"Textbook Unit 2 — Spacecraft electrical and thermal engineering","level":1,"sections":[["Why spacecraft need resource budgets","Spacecraft operate with limited power, mass, thermal rejection capability and communication opportunities. Electrical generation may depend on solar illumination, array pointing and component condition. Loads include avionics, radios, science instruments, heaters, pumps and actuators. A mission power budget states what operates, how much power it consumes, when it runs, and what margins are needed.","Power is measured in watts, while energy accumulated over time is measured in watt-hours or joules. Confusing these quantities leads to incorrect battery plans. A 20-watt load operating for three hours requires an ideal 60 watt-hours of electrical energy, before system losses or other simultaneous loads."],["Solar generation and orbital eclipses","A sunlit solar array produces energy only under suitable illumination and electrical conditions. Output changes with incidence angle, temperature, degradation, contamination and shading. In eclipse, a solar-powered spacecraft generally relies more heavily on stored energy. An energy balance must therefore consider the entire orbit or operational sequence, not just an instantaneous sunny-period reading.","Generating surplus power in sunlight can recharge batteries, but this depends on adequate time, charge efficiency, temperature and system limits. The mission may also schedule high-power activities around communication windows or favorable illumination, which connects the electrical subsystem to trajectory, attitude and operations planning."],["Thermal paths and spacecraft orientation","In vacuum, convection to surrounding air is unavailable. Radiation and conduction through spacecraft structures are important heat-transfer mechanisms. Sunlight and internal electronic dissipation are sources of heat; radiators reject heat by emitting radiation. Surface finishes, insulation and orientation influence thermal balance.","A spacecraft may need to point an antenna toward Earth, a telescope toward a target and a solar array toward the Sun. These orientations can conflict, introducing engineering tradeoffs. Thermal and power limits can become reasons to postpone an otherwise scientifically useful activity."],["Failure analysis and margins","A changing bus voltage may arise from load transitions, charging, regulation, sensor faults or genuine hardware degradation. Two current readings may be correlated because they share a measurement pathway. Engineers compare multiple independent signals, expected operating modes and synchronized timestamps to build competing hypotheses.","A healthy budget explicitly states uncertainty. Battery nameplate capacity is not always usable capacity; temperatures, aging, reserves and converter efficiency matter. A simple arithmetic model is valuable when its assumptions are visible, but it does not replace hardware specifications or validated mission analysis."]],"worked":"A fictional payload draws 35 W for two hours and an auxiliary load draws 10 W for one hour. Ideal combined energy is (35 × 2) + (10 × 1) = 80 Wh. If an assumed converter efficiency is 80%, the source must provide about 80/0.8 = 100 Wh, ignoring additional loads. Explain why this arithmetic still needs battery and temperature constraints.","optionalPrompt":"Sketch a sunlight-eclipse-recovery timeline showing load, generation, battery use and thermal tradeoffs. Identify which assumptions a mission engineer must validate.","questions":[["A 35 W payload operating for 2 hours uses how much ideal energy?",["70 Wh","17.5 Wh","35 joules"],0,"35 watts × 2 hours = 70 watt-hours."]]},{"module":2,"title":"Textbook Unit 5 — Mission design and systems engineering","level":1,"sections":[["From science objective to requirement","A mission begins with an objective, such as imaging a region or returning a measurement over a specified interval. That objective must be translated into measurable requirements. 'Provide images' is vague; a requirement specifying coverage, resolution, timing and applicable conditions can be verified.","Requirements impose demands on instruments, orbit design, power, communication, thermal control and schedule. Improving one subsystem can increase mass or electrical needs elsewhere, so engineers analyze linked tradeoffs."],["Budgets and constraints","Mission budgets account for mass, power, data, propellant, thermal capacity and available operations time. Each budget needs margins and assumptions. A spacecraft cannot simultaneously operate every desired instrument if electrical or thermal limits are exceeded.","Budgets should cover mission phases, including eclipse and communication gaps. Changes in one resource can alter the feasibility of an apparently independent scientific activity."],["Verification, validation and readiness","Verification asks whether a system meets stated requirements. Validation asks whether the resulting system fulfills the intended mission need. Tests, analysis, inspection and demonstrations provide different forms of evidence.","A readiness review identifies open risks, unresolved tests and decision authority. A successful demonstration in one condition should not be extrapolated to all operating conditions without justification."],["Planning for alternatives","Missions encounter missed communications windows, sensor anomalies, delayed activities and changed environmental conditions. A contingency plan should identify triggers, safe alternatives and how new evidence enters the decision.","The strongest operations plan does not treat a timeline as sacred. Documented hold points preserve flexibility and help teams avoid proceeding when important prerequisites are unknown."]],"worked":"A spacecraft can downlink 3 megabits per second during an 8-minute pass. Ignoring overhead and outages, theoretical maximum transfer is 3×480=1,440 megabits, or 180 megabytes using 8 bits per byte. Actual delivered science data will generally be lower because of protocol and operational constraints.","optionalPrompt":"Write three measurable mission requirements and the type of evidence required to verify each.","questions":[["What distinguishes verification from validation?",["Meeting requirements versus fulfilling the mission need","Both mean drawing a spacecraft","Validation only measures battery capacity"],0,"Verification checks stated requirements; validation addresses intended use and mission goals."]]},{"module":3,"title":"Textbook Unit 1 — Orbits, energy and mission maneuver reasoning","level":1,"objectives":["Apply circular orbital speed with dimensional awareness","Explain orbital energy and the shape of elliptical trajectories","Distinguish inertial from Earth-fixed frames","Interpret an ideal impulsive velocity change conceptually","Develop a telemetry-based maneuver verification plan"],"sections":[["The two-body approximation","A spacecraft moving under gravity follows a path governed by its initial state and the gravitational field. For a central body with gravitational parameter μ and a circular orbit of radius r, ideal circular speed is v = √(μ/r). The radius is measured from the central body's center, not from the surface. The units must be consistent: if μ is expressed in km³/s² and r in kilometers, the result is km/s. This equation assumes a simplified two-body circular orbit, not an arbitrary spacecraft trajectory.","In low orbits around Earth, atmospheric drag and Earth's uneven mass distribution perturb motion. Third-body gravity can matter over longer time scales. Mission analysts therefore use a numerical propagator or more complete physical model when making actual predictions. An elegant equation is a starting point for reasoning, not a substitute for state estimation."],["Gravity, free fall and orbital motion","Orbital free fall means the spacecraft and objects inside it share a gravitationally accelerated trajectory. Gravity does not disappear merely because the spacecraft is above the atmosphere. An orbit can be understood as horizontal motion combined with continuous gravitational curvature. The direction of velocity continually changes, even if its magnitude remains constant in an ideal circular orbit.","A common beginner misconception is that spacecraft must constantly burn propellant to keep orbiting. In the simplified two-body case, no continual thrust is required to remain on the trajectory. Real low orbits experience drag and may require occasional maneuvers, but that is different from powering every second of motion."],["Energy and elliptical orbits","Specific mechanical orbital energy in an ideal two-body model is ε = v²/2 − μ/r. For a bound Keplerian ellipse, ε = −μ/(2a), where a is semi-major axis. An impulse changes velocity and energy, which generally changes orbital shape. Prograde thrust at a low point of an orbit often raises the high point, rather than causing the spacecraft to fly radially outward in a straight line.","The vis-viva equation v² = μ(2/r − 1/a) links speed, radius and semi-major axis for a Keplerian orbit. Together these relationships help explain why spacecraft speeds vary around an ellipse. Real mission planning must consider maneuver duration, pointing accuracy, available propulsion, navigation covariance and correction strategy."],["Reference frames and timestamps","An inertial frame describes movement with a nonrotating reference approximation, whereas Earth-fixed frames rotate with Earth's surface. Ground track, orbital state, and communications geometry may be reported in different frames. Comparing numerical velocity vectors without checking frame definitions and measurement epochs can create apparent contradictions.","Telemetry may also arrive delayed or out of order. A robust operations system preserves time tags, units, calibration status and uncertainty. The analyst should never infer a maneuver outcome from a single impressive-looking number when other sensors or tracking information might disagree."],["How to verify a conceptual maneuver","A planning exercise can define a pre-maneuver state, a predicted post-maneuver orbit and a list of measurable indicators: tracking residuals, estimated orbital parameters, propellant status, thruster event data and navigation updates. Predictions should include ranges, because uncertainty is unavoidable. A go/no-go gate requires sufficient confidence that navigation, power, thermal and communication constraints are satisfied.","If post-maneuver telemetry differs from the prediction, investigators should consider both propulsion and sensing explanations. They should not assume a failed thruster or successful burn from the first reading. A structured debrief distinguishes observation, inferred change, uncertainty and next needed evidence."],["From textbook formulas to software","A computational lab can represent an orbit as position and velocity vectors and update them in small time steps. Numerical integration introduces approximation error, so results depend on step size and method. Comparing the computed trajectory against a known circular-orbit solution is one way to test a basic simulator. Conservation of energy and angular momentum under ideal two-body conditions are additional checks.","This academy's existing mission-control simulations are educational prototypes. Their animation and scoring should be evaluated against explicitly stated equations before being treated as reliable models. Programming, unit testing and uncertainty analysis are therefore integral parts of an advanced space-operations curriculum."]],"worked":"Worked circular-orbit calculation: for a fictional planet with μ = 400,000 km³/s² and r = 8,000 km, v = √(400,000/8,000) = √50 ≈ 7.07 km/s. If r increases to 10,000 km, v = √40 ≈ 6.32 km/s. This is a two-body classroom example; it omits perturbations. The change in ideal circular speed does not mean a spacecraft can move to the higher orbit by instantly reducing velocity at the lower orbit.","questions":[["Ideal circular orbital speed is proportional to:",["The inverse square root of radius","The square of radius","The altitude measured from sea level alone"],0,"With fixed μ, v=√(μ/r)."]],"optionalPrompt":"Write a 250–400-word orbital maneuver analysis describing the initial circular orbit, a conceptual prograde burn, expected ellipse, units, reference frame, energy change and telemetry required to verify the result. State at least four idealizations or uncertainties."},{"module":4,"title":"Textbook Unit 6 — Launch systems and ascent physics","level":1,"sections":[["Why rockets accelerate","A rocket produces thrust by expelling reaction mass. As propellant is consumed, vehicle mass falls, so acceleration can change even if thrust remains similar. Gravity, aerodynamic forces and commanded attitude influence the actual trajectory.","A launcher's ability to reach orbit is not captured by engine thrust alone. The trajectory and total velocity change depend on propulsion performance, mass ratio, gravity loss, atmospheric drag and guidance."],["Mass ratio and ideal delta-v","The ideal rocket equation relates velocity change to effective exhaust velocity and the logarithm of the initial-to-final mass ratio. This relationship illuminates why propellant mass dominates many rocket designs, and why structural mass and payload are important constraints.","Real launch trajectories incur gravity and aerodynamic losses; no single ideal number guarantees reaching orbit. This equation is an analytic teaching model that must be interpreted within a full flight dynamics framework."],["Staging as a systems tradeoff","Staging can discard expended tanks and engines, improving the mass ratio of the remaining stages. It introduces separation hardware, electrical and mechanical interfaces, control transitions and additional failure modes.","A flight-stage transition must be analyzed as a timed event with dependencies. Evidence from several telemetry sources is more reliable than assuming that a command has been executed simply because it was sent."],["Telemetry and ascent safety","Launch monitoring includes motion estimates, propulsion measurements, structural loads, temperatures and event status. Sample rates, delays and sensor quality affect interpretation. A single anomalous temperature or pressure reading does not prove a specific fault.","Risk and uncertainty must be communicated clearly. The educational simulator may model only a subset of real ascent physics, so its animations are best used to illustrate principles rather than represent certified launch operations."]],"worked":"For a hypothetical stage with effective exhaust velocity 3 km/s and mass ratio 2, ideal Δv = 3 ln(2) ≈ 2.08 km/s. This excludes external losses and reserves, and is not a vehicle-specific launch capability.","optionalPrompt":"Draw a conceptual two-stage mass-and-energy diagram, noting what the ideal rocket equation excludes.","questions":[["Which factors determine ideal rocket-equation delta-v?",["Effective exhaust velocity and mass ratio","Only rocket height","Only launchpad latitude"],0,"The ideal equation includes effective exhaust velocity and the logarithm of mass ratio."]]},{"module":5,"title":"Textbook Unit 3 — Telemetry, uncertainty and anomaly diagnosis","level":1,"sections":[["What telemetry actually measures","Telemetry reports values from sensors and onboard processing: voltage, current, temperature, attitude, propellant conditions, computer status and more. Each channel has units, sample rate, calibration, timing and availability constraints. A plotted line is evidence of recorded readings, not direct proof of the physical cause behind them.","Sensors can drift or fail, processing may filter or combine measurements, and communication links can delay or drop packets. Engineers need to understand a measurement pathway before interpreting a surprising value. A display that looks precise can still be inaccurate or misleading."],["Chronology and correlation","A mission timeline may contain command events, expected system responses, instrument readouts and communication gaps. If channel clocks are offset, an apparently earlier temperature rise may in fact have occurred after a power transition. Synchronizing timestamps is therefore a prerequisite for sound causal reasoning.","Even correctly ordered events do not automatically establish causality. Temperature and current may change together because a planned payload was activated; alternatively, an unrelated disturbance may influence both. A good analysis tests several hypotheses rather than committing to the first plausible story."],["Hypotheses and independent evidence","A hypothesis should predict measurable consequences. If a battery is genuinely losing capacity, analysts might expect recurring changes in voltage behavior under comparable loads; if one sensor is drifting, independent measurements may remain stable. Compare these expectations across operating modes and check for shared sensor or processing dependencies.","The best next observation is often one that distinguishes hypotheses. Collecting more readings of a single suspect channel may add volume without improving certainty. Document uncertainty and avoid treating a classification label such as 'power failure' as an established fact until evidence supports it."],["Decision gates and escalation","Mission operations require a record of known facts, unknowns, risk, available alternatives and authorization. A hold or safe-mode decision may depend on the severity of a possible condition and the confidence of the diagnosis. Real decisions are spacecraft-specific and rely on documented procedures, independent review and authority structures.","A learning simulation can present an anomaly and allow students to choose what evidence to inspect next. Its value lies in the reasoning process and transparent consequences, not a promise of professional operations certification."]],"worked":"A channel reports current rising at 10:00, voltage decreasing at 10:01 and a thermal sensor changing at 10:02. Another sensor is sampled every five minutes. Explain why these timestamps alone cannot prove which event caused another, and identify a next independent measurement that could help.","optionalPrompt":"Create an optional anomaly table with observations, competing causes, expected corroboration, confidence, and a justified follow-up measurement.","questions":[["Why is timestamp alignment important when analyzing telemetry?",["Timing differences can create a false event sequence","It corrects every calibration error","It proves one signal caused another"],0,"Accurate chronology matters, but correlation is still not sufficient for causality."]]},{"module":1,"title":"Level 2 — Spacecraft energy balances and eclipse operations","level":2,"sections":[["Instantaneous power versus energy over time","Power budgets must distinguish watts from watt-hours. A 100-watt load operates at a rate of 100 joules per second; over two hours its ideal energy use is 200 watt-hours. Spacecraft engineers consider both total energy and peak load, because a battery may have ample energy yet still be unable to deliver a particular power level safely.","Loads change by mission phase: an instrument may be idle during eclipse, a radio may transmit during a ground contact, and heaters may operate when temperature falls. A single average number cannot reveal all instantaneous constraints."],["Generation, orientation and eclipse","Solar-array output depends on illumination, angle to the Sun, thermal characteristics, aging and electrical conversion. When the vehicle enters eclipse, direct solar generation drops and stored energy becomes critical. The length and timing of eclipse vary with orbit and mission geometry.","A daylight recharge estimate must allow for concurrent loads and losses. Pointing a payload or antenna may also constrain the array orientation, creating a schedule tradeoff between data gathering, communications and electrical recovery."],["Battery margins and state of charge","Nominal battery capacity does not equal guaranteed usable capacity. Discharge-rate limits, temperature, degradation, reserve policy and conversion losses change actual capability. An energy balance should show starting storage, generation, consumption and expected end state over each phase.","A modeled positive energy balance is not proof that every subsystem will remain within its voltage or thermal limits. Battery capability should be checked against hardware specifications and telemetry."],["Thermal-electrical coupling","In vacuum, radiation and conduction dominate spacecraft heat transfer rather than ordinary air convection. Internal electronics generate heat while sunlight adds thermal input. Heaters draw electrical power to protect components; radiators reject heat through emission.","The power plan and thermal plan therefore cannot be developed independently. A change in pointing or payload operating time may affect both temperature and battery state."],["Operations with uncertainty","A strong budget explicitly states uncertain loads, generation conditions and margins. If observed telemetry differs from prediction, controllers should compare independent measurements and the timing of known events before assigning cause.","This academy uses examples to teach system reasoning. Actual spacecraft operations require validated models, test evidence and mission-specific procedures."]],"worked":"A fictional spacecraft consumes 80 Wh during eclipse and its battery-to-load chain is modeled at 80% efficiency. Ideal battery-side demand is 80/0.8 = 100 Wh before reserve and temperature effects. If the battery begins with 200 Wh usable under stated assumptions, this single phase uses about half that amount. The result does not guarantee a real system can support the peak load.","optionalPrompt":"Sketch a three-phase solar-eclipse-recharge energy table and identify two independent telemetry channels that could corroborate the predicted battery change.","questions":[["Why is a spacecraft eclipse budget based on energy rather than only power?",["Loads consume energy over an eclipse duration","Watts and watt-hours mean the same thing","Solar arrays always generate at night"],0,"Energy demand depends on power multiplied by operating time."]]},{"module":2,"title":"Level 2 — Requirements traceability and mission decision gates","level":2,"sections":[["Requirements as testable statements","An objective such as 'study surface changes' must be refined into measurement requirements, observation schedules, data quality needs and transmission capacity. A requirement is stronger when it names an observable performance criterion and the conditions of evaluation.","Traceability connects each mission objective to system functions, tests and evidence. If a requirement has no test, the team cannot demonstrate compliance. If a test has no mission justification, it may consume resources without answering a relevant question."],["Mass, power, data and time budgets","Subsystems compete for constrained resources. High-resolution instruments may produce more data but also draw more power and create thermal demands. Communications capacity depends on available passes, link performance, ground assets and operational overhead.","Mission-level tradeoffs need a common representation of assumptions. A power budget with generous margins cannot compensate for an impossible communications schedule, and a perfect data plan is unhelpful if the vehicle cannot point the instrument."],["Verification versus validation","Verification asks whether the implemented system meets its specified requirements. Validation asks whether those requirements and resulting behavior serve the intended mission. Both are necessary: a technically compliant camera may still be unsuitable for a scientific objective.","Evidence can come from analysis, test, inspection or demonstration, each with limits. A test in one environmental condition does not automatically validate performance in all other states."],["Operations readiness and hold points","A mission timeline should identify preconditions, decision authority and fallback paths. Examples include adequate navigation certainty, sufficient electrical reserve, known attitude constraints and available communications. A go/no-go review makes missing evidence visible.","Proceeding despite an unresolved prerequisite can increase operational risk. A hold, reschedule or alternative activity may preserve the mission's objectives better than completing the planned timeline at any cost."],["Reviewing anomalies without losing the objective","An anomaly may demand a temporary change to the operations plan. Clear traceability helps show what mission capabilities are affected and what can still be done safely. Document whether assumptions changed and what evidence would support resuming the activity.","The learning goal is systems-level judgment: protect essential resources, avoid unjustified certainty and connect every decision to a measurable mission need."]],"worked":"An imagined payload produces 500 MB of data during a pass, but a downlink can sustainably transfer 6 megabits/s for 8 minutes. Ignoring overhead, the link sends 6×480 = 2,880 megabits = 360 MB (using 8 bits/byte). The shortfall is at least 140 MB in this simplified model. Storage, another pass or a revised observation plan would be needed.","optionalPrompt":"Create a traceability table from three scientific objectives to measurable requirements, data products, test evidence and contingency plans.","questions":[["A mission requirement is most useful when it is:",["Measurable and traceable to evidence","An inspirational slogan","Independent of system constraints"],0,"Verification needs defined conditions and measurable evidence."]]},{"module":3,"title":"Level 2 — Orbit transfers, impulses and navigation confidence","level":2,"sections":[["Energy and orbital geometry","A bound two-body Keplerian orbit is characterized by size and shape as well as orientation. The semi-major axis relates to specific orbital energy via ε = −μ/(2a). For a circular orbit, a = r, while for an ellipse the radius changes continuously along the path.","A spacecraft does not move directly outward just because a maneuver increases its speed. The added velocity changes energy and angular momentum, producing a new trajectory whose future geometry must be predicted."],["An ideal prograde impulse","A velocity increment along the direction of flight at a circular orbit location generally raises the opposite side of the resulting elliptical orbit. The vehicle remains at the burn location at the instant of the impulse; the orbital path changes around that point.","This is a useful conceptual model of orbital transfers. Real spacecraft use finite-duration thrust, and maneuver effects depend on timing, thrust direction, mass, attitude and navigation uncertainty."],["The vis-viva relation","The two-body equation v² = μ(2/r − 1/a) relates speed, current radius and semi-major axis. It can describe speeds along a Keplerian ellipse as well as circular motion when a = r. The units of μ, r and a must be consistent.","A larger semi-major axis changes orbital period and energy. A transfer plan therefore considers both geometry and scheduling rather than attempting to compare isolated speed values from different orbit locations."],["Navigation and reference frames","Maneuver design requires knowledge of spacecraft position and velocity at a defined time in a particular reference frame. A wrong timestamp or mixed frame convention can turn correct equations into wrong operational conclusions. State-vector covariance describes uncertainty and can grow between tracking opportunities.","Tracking data, propulsion telemetry and independent orbit estimates should be compared after a maneuver. An observed change is not enough without checking whether it matches a predicted change within reasonable uncertainty."],["Conservation checks and model limitations","In an ideal unpowered two-body orbit, specific mechanical energy and angular momentum remain constant. A numerical model that shows large unexplained changes may contain integration errors, unit mistakes or a mismatch in assumptions.","Real effects such as atmospheric drag and third-body gravity can change these quantities. Simulation quality improves when the simplified model is validated first, and additional effects are introduced carefully."]],"worked":"For a fictional body with μ=400,000 km³/s², at circular radius r=8,000 km the speed is √(μ/r)=√50≈7.07 km/s. A hypothetical elliptical orbit with a=10,000 km has speed at r=8,000 km of √[400,000(2/8,000−1/10,000)] = √60≈7.75 km/s. This is a conceptual velocity comparison, not a complete burn prescription.","optionalPrompt":"Compare a circular reference orbit and an elliptical transfer using a labeled diagram showing the burn point, semi-major axis and two expected tracking indicators.","questions":[["What does a prograde impulse generally alter?",["Orbital energy and trajectory geometry","The existence of gravity","The spacecraft's location instantly by thousands of kilometers"],0,"An impulse changes velocity and hence the subsequent orbit, not the position at that instant."]]}]};